-- THE SECOND CONTROL ON THE ACCEPTED HALF OF T-FIELD-VARIANT-CONFLICT: a sibling
-- constructor that declares a DIFFERENT field is not a rival (#1455 second review,
-- finding F4).
--
-- `T` has two named-field constructors, but only `A` declares `x`, so a `.x`
-- selection has exactly one candidate offset and the guard must not fire.  Without
-- this cell nothing in the tree pins that axis: a future change that widened the
-- rule to "any multi-constructor receiver" would pass every other fixture here.
-- (Round 3 of #1455 deleted the only control that covered a neighbouring axis and
-- replaced it with a single-CONSTRUCTOR one, which cannot see this.)
--
-- 🚨 THIS CELL PINS ACCEPTANCE, NOT CORRECTNESS, AND THE DIFFERENCE IS AN OPEN S0.
-- `getX (B { z = 99 })` has no meaning, and the built binary happily reads `B`'s
-- slot 0 for it — a wrong `Int` at exit 0, or, with the sibling field `String`-typed,
-- a heap address printed as an `Int`.  MEASURED identically on the merge base
-- `bfcd4ea7` and on this branch, so it is pre-existing and NOT drained by #1455;
-- it is filed as **#1468** and pinned in `test/must_fail_fixtures/`.  What this cell
-- asserts is only that #1455's guard does not fire here — which is correct, because
-- the offsets do not disagree, and because rejecting it would reject `getX mkA` too.
--
-- ⚠️ THE ASSERTION IS THE ERROR THAT DOES APPEAR, NOT THE ABSENCE OF ONE.  The body
-- is ill-typed in an UNRELATED way (`Bool` annotation, `Int` field), so the golden is
-- a one-line `Type mismatch` that can only be produced if `.x` RESOLVED.
data T =
  | A { x : Int }
  | B { z : Int }

getX : T -> Bool
getX t = t.x
